1
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:09,159
The early summer days on a farm are the happiest and fairest days of the year.

2
00:00:09,159 --> 00:00:14,280
Lilacs bloom and make the air sweet and then fade.

3
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:19,400
Apple blossoms come with the lilacs and the bees visit around among the apple

4
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:23,400
trees. The days grow warm and soft.

5
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:27,960
School ends and children have time to play and to fish for trouts in the

6
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:33,880
brook. Avery often brought a trout home in his pocket, warm and stiff and ready

7
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:37,239
to be fried for supper.

8
00:00:37,239 --> 00:00:42,440
Now that school was over, Fern visited the barn almost every day to sit quietly

9
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:50,280
on her stool. The animals treated her as an equal. The sheep lay calmly at her feet.

10
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:54,838
Around the 1st of July, the workh horses were hitched to the mowing machine, and

11
00:00:54,838 --> 00:01:01,000
Mr. Zuckerman climbed into the seat and drove into the field. All morning you

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,639
could hear the rattle of the machine as it went round and round while the tall

13
00:01:05,639 --> 00:01:12,358
grass fell down behind the cutter bar in long green swaths.

14
00:01:12,358 --> 00:01:17,798
Next day, if there was no thunderher, all hands would help rake and pitch and

15
00:01:17,798 --> 00:01:22,920
load, and the hay would be hauled to the barn in the high hay wagon, with Fern

16
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:26,280
and Avery riding at the top of the load.

17
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:32,838
Then the hay would be hoisted, sweet and warm, into the big loft, until the whole

18
00:01:32,838 --> 00:01:39,480
barn seemed like a wonderful bed of Timothy and Clover. It was fine to jump

19
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:45,798
in and perfect to hide in. And sometimes Avery would find a little grass snake in

20
00:01:45,798 --> 00:01:51,318
the hay and would add it to the other things in his pocket.

21
00:01:51,318 --> 00:01:57,798
Early summer days are a jubilee time for birds. In the fields, around the house,

22
00:01:57,798 --> 00:02:04,920
in the barn, in the woods, in the swamp, everywhere love and songs and nests and

23
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:11,079
eggs. From the edge of the woods, the white-throatated sparrow, which must

24
00:02:11,079 --> 00:02:18,239
come all the way from Boston, calls, "Oh, Peabody, Peabody, Peabody."

25
00:02:18,599 --> 00:02:27,599
On an apple bow, the Phoebe teeters and wags its tail and says, "Pee, Phoebe."

26
00:02:29,079 --> 00:02:35,480
The song Sparrow, who knows how brief and lovely life is, says, "Sweet, sweet,

27
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:41,039
sweet interlude. Sweet, sweet, sweet interlude."

28
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:50,199
If you enter the barn, the swallows swoop down from their nests and scold.

29
00:02:50,199 --> 00:02:54,639
"Cheeky cheeky," they say.

30
00:02:57,798 --> 00:03:03,079
In early summer, there are plenty of things for a child to eat and drink and

31
00:03:03,079 --> 00:03:09,400
suck and chew. Dandelion stems are full of milk. Clover heads are loaded with

32
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:15,719
nectar. The frigid air is full of ice cold drinks. Everywhere you look, is

33
00:03:15,719 --> 00:03:21,480
life. Even the little ball of spit on the weed stalk, if you poke it apart,

34
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:26,760
has a green worm inside it. And on the underside of the leaf of the potato vine

35
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:33,000
are the bright orange eggs of the potato bug. It was on a day in early summer

36
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:39,000
that the goose eggs hatched. This was an important event in the barn cellar. Fern

37
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,719
was there sitting on her stool when it happened.

38
00:03:43,719 --> 00:03:48,440
Except for the goose herself, Charlotte was the first to know that the gosslings

39
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:54,358
had at last arrived. The goose knew a day in advance that they were coming.

40
00:03:54,358 --> 00:04:00,358
She could hear their weak voices calling from inside the egg. She knew that they

41
00:04:00,358 --> 00:04:05,400
were in a desperately cramped position inside the shell and were most anxious

42
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:12,920
to break through and get out, so she sat quite still and talked less than usual.

43
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:17,639
When the first goling poked its gray green head through the goose's feathers

44
00:04:17,639 --> 00:04:23,639
and looked around, Charlotte spied it and made the announcement.

45
00:04:23,639 --> 00:04:29,798
"I am sure," she said, "that every one of us here will be gratified to learn

46
00:04:29,798 --> 00:04:35,798
that after 4 weeks of unremitting effort and patience on the part of our friend

47
00:04:35,798 --> 00:04:43,239
the goose, she now has something to show for it. The glings have arrived. May I

48
00:04:43,239 --> 00:04:47,239
offer my sincere congratulations?

49
00:04:47,239 --> 00:04:52,760
Oh, thank you, thank you, thank you, said the goose, nodding and bowing

50
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:56,920
shamelessly. Thank you, said the gender.

51
00:04:56,920 --> 00:05:04,440
Congratulations, shouted Wilbur. How many golings are there? I can only see one. There are

52
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:07,239
seven, said the goose.

53
00:05:07,239 --> 00:05:13,639
Fine, said Charlotte. Seven is a lucky number. Luck had nothing to do with

54
00:05:13,639 --> 00:05:21,159
this, said the goose. It was good management and hard work.

55
00:05:21,159 --> 00:05:26,679
At this point, Templeton showed his nose from his hiding place under Wilbur's

56
00:05:26,679 --> 00:05:32,838
trough. He glanced at Fern, then crept cautiously toward the goose, keeping

57
00:05:32,838 --> 00:05:41,239
close to the wall. Everyone watched him, for he was not well-liked, not trusted.

58
00:05:41,239 --> 00:05:48,519
Look, he began in his sharp voice. You say you have uh seven glings. There were

59
00:05:48,519 --> 00:05:55,719
eight eggs. What happened to the other egg? Uh why didn't it hatch? It's a dud,

60
00:05:55,719 --> 00:06:00,920
I guess, said the goose. What are you going to do with it? Uh," continued

61
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:07,480
Templeton, his little round beady eyes fixed on the goose.

62
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:10,838
"You can have it," replied the goose.

63
00:06:10,838 --> 00:06:16,440
"Roll it away and add it to that nasty collection of yours." Templeton had a

64
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:22,280
habit of picking up unusual objects around the farm and storing them in his

65
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:27,000
home. He saved everything.

66
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,358
Certainly, certainly, said the gander.

67
00:06:30,358 --> 00:06:37,480
You may have the egg, but I'll tell you one thing, Timon. If I ever catch you

68
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:47,239
poking, oaking, oaking your ugly nose around our glings, I'll give you the worst pounding a rat

69
00:06:47,239 --> 00:06:53,639
ever took. And the gander opened his strong wings and beat the air with them

70
00:06:53,639 --> 00:06:57,679
to show his power.

71
00:06:58,679 --> 00:07:05,400
He was strong and brave, but the truth is both the goose and the gander were

72
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:10,838
worried about Templeton, and with good reason.

73
00:07:10,838 --> 00:07:19,639
The rat had no morals, no conscience, no scruples, no consideration, no decency,

74
00:07:19,639 --> 00:07:27,000
no milk of rodent kindness, no compunctions, no higher feeling, no

75
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:33,798
friendliness, no anything. He would kill a glinging if he could get away with it.

76
00:07:33,798 --> 00:07:37,239
The goose knew that. Everybody knew it.

77
00:07:37,239 --> 00:07:42,519
With her broad bill, the goose pushed the unhatched egg out of the nest, and

78
00:07:42,519 --> 00:07:48,519
the entire company watched in disgust while the rat rolled it away. Even

79
00:07:48,519 --> 00:07:54,039
Wilbur, who could eat almost anything, was appalled. "Imagine wanting a chunky

80
00:07:54,039 --> 00:07:58,440
old rotten egg," he muttered.

81
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:08,440
"A rat is a rat," said Charlotte. She laughed a tinkling little laugh. But my friends, if that

82
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:13,639
ancient egg ever breaks, this barn will be untenable.

83
00:08:13,639 --> 00:08:19,719
What's that mean? asked Wilbur. It means nobody will be able to live here on

84
00:08:19,719 --> 00:08:27,239
account of the smell. A rotten egg is a regular stink bomb. I won't break it,

85
00:08:27,239 --> 00:08:32,199
snarled Templeton. I know what I'm doing. I handle stuff like this all the

86
00:08:32,199 --> 00:08:37,960
time. He disappeared into his tunnel, pushing the goose egg in front of him.

87
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:45,559
He pushed and nudged till he succeeded in rolling it to his layer under the trough.

88
00:08:45,559 --> 00:08:50,679
That afternoon, when the wind had died down, and the barnyard was quiet and

89
00:08:50,679 --> 00:08:56,838
warm, the gray goose led her seven gosslings off the nest and out into the

90
00:08:56,838 --> 00:09:03,079
world. Mr. Zuckerman spied them when he came with Wilbur's supper. Well, hello

91
00:09:03,079 --> 00:09:12,358
there," he said, smiling all over. Let's see. One, two, three, four, five, six,

92
00:09:12,358 --> 00:09:19,599
seven. Seven baby geese. Now, isn't that lovely?

93
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:22,838
Chapter 7.

94
00:09:22,838 --> 00:09:26,000
Bad news.
